Here's a JavaScript function that switches the order of adjectives in a sentence:

```javascript
function switchAdjectives(sentence) {
  const words = sentence.split(" ");
  const adjectives = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
    if (words[i].match(/^(\w+)\s+(\w+)$/)) {
      adjectives.push(words[i]);
    }
  }

  if (adjectives.length > 1) {
    const firstAdjective = adjectives.shift();
    const lastAdjective = adjectives.pop();
    adjectives.unshift(lastAdjective);
    adjectives.push(firstAdjective);

    const newSentence = words.join(" ");
    for (let i = 0; i < adjectives.length; i++) {
      newSentence = newSentence.replace(` ${adjectives[i]} `, ` ${adjectives[i]} `);
    }

    return newSentence;
  } else {
    return sentence;
  }
}

const sentence = "Beautiful green trees are in the park";
const result = switchAdjectives(sentence);
console.log(result); // Output: "Green beautiful trees are in the park"
```

This function splits the input sentence into an array of words, then finds all the adjective pairs by matching on a regular expression. It then reverses the order of the adjectives and replaces them in the original sentence. If there are no adjectives in the sentence, it returns the input sentence unchanged.

