Here's a simple React utility that removes widows (lines that are too long and wrap around) in text. This utility uses the `word-wrap` CSS property to wrap words within a specified maximum width. If a word is too long and wraps around, it will be removed from the text.

```javascript
import React from 'react';

const RemoveWidows = ({ text, maxWidth }) => {
  const words = text.split(' ');
  const newText = words.map((word) => {
    if (word.length > maxWidth) {
      return `${word.slice(0, maxWidth)}...`;
    }
    return word;
  });
  return newText.join(' ');
};

export default RemoveWidows;
```

To use this utility, you can import it into your React component and pass in the text and maximum width as props. For example:

```javascript
import React from 'react';
import RemoveWidows from './RemoveWidows';

const MyComponent = () => {
  const text = 'Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit. Sed non risus. Suspendisse lectus tortor, dignissim sed, adipiscing nec, ultricies sed, dolor. Cras mattis consectetur purus, sed tincidunt magna aliquet non. Ut a lacus sit amet lorem vestibulum adipiscing.';
  const maxWidth = 200;

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Remove Widows</h1>
      <p>{RemoveWidows(text, maxWidth)}</p>
    </div>
  );
};

export default MyComponent;
```

In this example, the `RemoveWidows` utility is used to remove widows from the given text with a maximum width of 200 characters. The result is displayed in a paragraph element.

